    <p>The 2020s have already demonstrated the continued capacity for oil markets to deliver extreme price movements. The COVID-19 collapse to <strong>$21.04/bbl</strong> (April 2020) was followed by a rapid recovery and a subsequent spike to <strong>$116.80/bbl</strong> (June 2022) driven by the Russia-Ukraine conflict. As of March 2026, prices have settled near <strong>${d['last_price']:.2f}/bbl</strong> &mdash; a level consistent with the post-shale, post-pandemic equilibrium but well below the 2022 peak.</p>

    <h3>Key Takeaways</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Long-term appreciation is structural, not linear.</strong> The {d['cagr']:.2f}% CAGR over 56 years masks multi-decade flat or declining periods (1980s&ndash;1990s, 2014&ndash;2016, 2020).</li>
        <li><strong>Volatility is endogenous to the market structure.</strong> Each major supply-control shift (OPEC cartelization, market liberalization, shale elasticity) has produced a distinct volatility signature.</li>
        <li><strong>Geopolitical risk premia are persistent but time-varying.</strong> The magnitude of price shocks has not diminished; rather, the market's recovery dynamics have accelerated due to financialization and information speed.</li>
